{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Elohim has endowed me with a good endowment</b>. They have found no precedent for this word in the sacred tongue. It is possible that it is said to be a compound word — זה בד (this is a portion), like the word מדוע and the word בגד (Genesis 30:11) in their written form, and the segol and the pataḥ are interchanged in many places, all the more so in compound words. Similarly the word צלמות and the word בלעדי. And it derives from the language of בדים, \"the poles of acacia wood\" (Exodus 25:13), \"and it made boughs and sent forth branches\" (Ezekiel 17:6) — for the thick branches of the tree are called thus. And they borrowed it for figurative use: \"the firstborn of death shall consume his boughs\" (Job 18:13), \"surely his boughs are not so\" (Isaiah 16:6). Therefore Leah said that Elohim had made this son for her as a good בד, to be a shelter in which her husband lodges, for he will bestow his habitation (יזבלני) upon me. Similarly, \"I have surely built you a house of habitation (זבול)\" (I Kings 8:13) — to lodge in its shade; and for the honor of the Most High one says thus, as he said (ibid. v. 27), \"Behold, the heavens and the heaven of heavens cannot contain You, much less this house that I have built.\" And similarly, \"Sun and moon stood still in their habitation (זבולה)\" (Habakkuk 3:11) — they stood in the place where they lodge. As it says, \"He has set a tent for the sun\" (Psalms 19:5). And Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 30:20) translated זבד טוב as חולק טב (a good portion), and I did not understand his reasoning until I saw in the Targum of Jonathan (Targum Yerushalmi) זויד יתי מימרא דה׳ זודין טבין, and I learned that they rendered the word as though it were written with a vav, and they attached it to the Aramaic tongue in which צידה is translated זודין (Onkelos on Genesis 42:25). She said that the Name had made this son for her as good provision and a good portion, for her husband's principal dwelling would be with her because of her many children, so she would be sustained together with him in the good. And the vav and the bet are equivalent in our language in many places, such as תאוה and תאבה (Psalms 119:20), גוי נתתי למכים (Isaiah 50:6), על גבי חרשו חרשים (Psalms 129:3), and similarly לקצבי הרים ירדתי (Jonah 2:7) like לקצוי, and לפרבר למערב ארבעה (I Chronicles 26:18) — they say it is from פרור, as it is written (II Kings 23:11) \"to the chamber of Nathan-melech the eunuch, which was in the פרורים,\" and the meaning of both is what is outside the wall, outward; מגרש לערים (Numbers 35:2), the Jerusalem Targum renders ופרור, and so Targum Jonathan on מגרשות (Ezekiel 27:28). And in the Mishnah: מלא תרוד, תרבד (Nazir 49a, Oholot 2:1); בטבלא שאין לה לבזבז (Pesaḥim 48a) — in some places they read from it לזבז (Kelim 2:3, Mikva'ot 4:2); and in some places they read לזויז; אצטבא (Pesaḥim 11a), איצטוא (Eruvin 77a, Yoma 49a according to the reading of the Arukh), and many similar cases. And in the Jerusalem Talmud they are accustomed to say אביר with a bet in place of a vav (Kil'ayim 5:29, Yevamot 7:8, and elsewhere), for they are not particular about this."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "Ramban reads זבד as related to זבול (dwelling/lodging) and traces a web of phonetic interchanges (vav/bet, segol/pataḥ, ז/b alternations) across biblical and rabbinic Hebrew to support his etymology; he reports initially not understanding Onkelos until seeing Targum Jonathan, which clarifies Onkelos's rendering as Aramaic זודין = צידה (provision)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:37:17.381069892Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}